<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</title><subtitle type="html">Deutsche Welle: DW.com - Germany</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-ger</id><updated>2026-11-11T06:47:54Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss-en-ger" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-1432?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss-en-ger" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>Who is Ilse Aigner, leading candidate for Germany's next president?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ba31fbe6a031958fb152f10f6faf6e892a6cd42773ef814982c9700174b143b0</id><updated>2026-11-11T06:47:54Z</updated><published>2026-11-09T11:11:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/who-is-ilse-aigner-leading-candidate-for-germany-s-next-president/a-79898848--5540ac90be2d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/who-is-ilse-aigner-leading-candidate-for-germany-s-next-president/a-79898848?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">She would be the first female president and the first Christian Social Union politician to hold Germany's highest office. Ilse Aigner is the favored candidate for the Federal Convention vote on January 30.</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>Germany news: Ilse Aigner meets parties at Bundestag</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a2002d26919390242377293d6d5c4bc7dc5be696cf49facf56908fd0db9e599f</id><updated>2026-11-11T06:47:54Z</updated><published>2026-11-10T08:36:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-news-ilse-aigner-meets-parties-at-bundestag/live-79906440--d4a05b14149e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-ilse-aigner-meets-parties-at-bundestag/live-79906440?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Ilse Aigner said she wouldn't meet with the far-right AfD. Meanwhile, Chancellor Merz is set to host Hungarian Prime Minister Magyar.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany: CDU names Ilse Aigner as candidate for president</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1bd54e0d896bda52ad1f000134a91fdc514e97d539510ce973a5d0b1986dbd6b</id><updated>2026-11-11T06:47:54Z</updated><published>2026-11-09T12:11:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-cdu-names-ilse-aigner-as-candidate-for-president/a-79898143--f4c7d81c5dcc.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-cdu-names-ilse-aigner-as-candidate-for-president/a-79898143?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The steering committtee of the CDU have announced Bavarian politician Ilse Aigner as presidential candidate. This gives her a good chance of becoming the first woman to occupy the role.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Germany news: Germany marks fateful date of November 9</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4ee791b075a0de8f0404be3cec7e47cf0f69460ede984bc5626832cade9bae07</id><updated>2026-11-11T06:47:54Z</updated><published>2026-11-09T07:43:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-news-germany-marks-fateful-date-of-november-9/live-79895613--b598062ac50d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-germany-marks-fateful-date-of-november-9/live-79895613?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Germany is commemorating the Nazi-led November pogroms 88 years ago. Events are also being held to mark the anniversary of the fall of the Berlin Wall in 1989. DW has more.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>Controversial kinship: From colonial rule to the Holocaust?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8f76c7e05c11313d30e8d7067d1e8d931963f44093b88ccbdc62e92e4efa39c5</id><updated>2026-11-11T06:47:54Z</updated><published>2026-11-10T15:49:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/controversial-kinship-from-colonial-rule-to-the-holocaust/a-79902228--16129c6452e2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/controversial-kinship-from-colonial-rule-to-the-holocaust/a-79902228?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Hitler once called Eastern Europe "Germany's India." A new exhibition examines the links between colonialism and Nazism, and the bitter debate over where comparisons illuminate history and where they fall short.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Neuer injury gives Urbig chance to shape Bayern and Germany future</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f8b689b830b9c5f19a029dc3e754cd4e2eecf6afe9d2aa587630d2b78b64ef63</id><updated>2026-11-11T06:47:54Z</updated><published>2026-11-10T15:13:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/neuer-injury-gives-urbig-chance-to-shape-bayern-and-germany-future/a-79910042--b0a77bac4246.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/neuer-injury-gives-urbig-chance-to-shape-bayern-and-germany-future/a-79910042?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Manuel Neuer's latest injury gives Jonas Urbig a run of games in Bayern's goal. Marc-André ter Stegen remains Germany’s No. 1, but Urbig now has a chance to strengthen his case for the future.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>Borussia Dortmund's Felix Nmecha and the question of religion</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:c21ec9fe244fd2daa7e3266756aa8055f6c3ccad461eec875013fc70537d7338</id><updated>2026-11-11T06:47:54Z</updated><published>2026-11-10T11:12:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/borussia-dortmund-s-felix-nmecha-and-the-question-of-religion/a-79900432--cd7bb6e11389.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/borussia-dortmund-s-felix-nmecha-and-the-question-of-religion/a-79900432?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Felix Nmecha's "Crowns down" celebration has drawn criticism from a Borussia Dortmund fan club, reopening debate over faith, inclusion and religious messaging in football.</summary><category term="Sports" /></entry><entry><title>The dachshund, a humorous icon with political symbolism</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:fcad094f4a9f7f57f486e175adf4a7fddc27e16b2628019f32c6322923422652</id><updated>2026-11-11T06:47:54Z</updated><published>2026-10-30T07:57:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/the-dachshund-a-humorous-icon-with-political-symbolism/a-79640191--0ec0144d606e.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/the-dachshund-a-humorous-icon-with-political-symbolism/a-79640191?maca=en-rss-en-ger-1023-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">From Picasso to Andy Warhol, a surprising number of artists have portrayed the German breed. But the dachshund has also at times been co-opted as a political symbol.</summary><category term="Culture" /></entry></feed>
